<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  CardDemo Auth Service : Maven module descriptor
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  Purpose:
    Build descriptor for the auth bounded context of the CardDemo mainframe to
    AWS migration. The context carries sign-on plus user administration and is
    the migration target of five COBOL programs: COSGN00C (sign-on), COUSR00C
    (user list), COUSR01C (add user), COUSR02C (update user) and COUSR03C
    (delete user). It owns the PostgreSQL schema named auth and ships exactly
    one Flyway migration, V1__auth.sql.

  Inherits, from com.carddemo:carddemo-services, itself parented to
  org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-parent:
    * every dependency version, by way of three imported BOMs plus the
      coordinates the aggregator manages explicitly;
    * the Java release, and the source and reporting encodings;
    * the Checkstyle execution bound to the Maven validate phase;
    * spring-boot-starter-test at test scope, declared once in the aggregator's
      own dependencies element and therefore present in all nine modules;
    * the Surefire and Failsafe split, which routes classes carrying a Test
      suffix to the test phase and classes carrying an IT suffix to
      integration-test and verify, writing into Maven's default report
      directories because the services CI workflow collects exactly those paths.

  Contributes:
    * the compile classpath this module actually uses, enumerated below;
    * a container-backed test scope for the repository integration test;
    * a bootable jar, produced by spring-boot-maven-plugin, which this module's
      Dockerfile copies as a single artifact.

  Gates:
    The inherited Checkstyle documentation gate runs at validate, so it fires on
    every local build, every CI build, and every container image build of this
    module. Nothing in this file relaxes it. The written convention it enforces
    is docs/CODE_DOCUMENTATION_STANDARD.md; the ruleset itself is
    config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml, located through a property the aggregator
    defines and this file deliberately does not redefine.

  WHY (non-obvious design decisions):

    Assumptions: not one dependency below carries a version element, because the
      aggregator's dependencyManagement supplies every one of them. Re-stating a
      version here would create a second place to edit, and the two copies would
      then drift apart silently at the next upgrade rather than failing loudly.

    Trade-offs: this project pins exact versions rather than ranges, which costs
      a deliberate edit to accept any upgrade. That cost is accepted because the
      repository verifies migrated behaviour against byte-deterministic golden
      masters, so a floating dependency could shift a comparison result with no
      change to any source file. The same doctrine, and the same reasoning, is
      recorded for the Python test harness in tests/README.md near line 180. It
      is paraphrased here rather than quoted, for the reason in the next note.

    Assumptions: XML 1.0 section 2.5 forbids two adjacent hyphen characters
      anywhere inside a comment, on pain of the document not parsing at all.
      Every horizontal rule in this file is therefore drawn with tildes, and the
      command line flags named in the sources cited above are described in words
      instead of being transcribed. A future reader who restores a hyphen rule
      for visual consistency with this repository's other annotated files will
      make this file unreadable to Maven, and the build will not start.

    Alternatives Considered: maven-checkstyle-plugin is not re-declared in this
      module. Configuring it per module was weighed and rejected, because the
      aggregator already binds it for every module, and a second binding would
      let the nine modules drift onto nine different rulesets. The suppressions
      file is likewise not wired from here: checkstyle.xml pulls it in through
      its own SuppressionFilter module, resolving the path against ${config_loc}
      and declaring it non-optional so that a missing file stops the build.
      Naming the same file again from Maven would create a competing wiring, and
      the two could disagree about which suppressions are in force.

    Assumptions: this module deliberately carries no .gitignore of its own. The
      repository root already ignores target/ with an intentionally unanchored
      pattern, chosen because Maven output only ever appears nested beneath
      services/<module>/, so that single root policy protects this module from
      its very first build onward.
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
    WHAT: the coordinates that place this module inside the nine module reactor,
          second in build order, immediately after common-lib.
    WHY : Assumptions: the artifact identifier below has to match the aggregator's
          own module entry character for character, because Maven correlates the
          two by string equality and reports a module it cannot correlate as
          missing rather than as misnamed. The relative path is stated explicitly
          so that a build launched from inside this directory finds the parent on
          disk instead of reaching out to a repository for it, which is what
          keeps a single module build behaving the same as a reactor build.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
    <artifactId>carddemo-services</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>auth-service</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>CardDemo Auth Service</name>
  <description>
    Sign-on and user administration bounded context of the CardDemo migration,
    carrying the behaviour of COBOL programs COSGN00C, COUSR00C, COUSR01C,
    COUSR02C and COUSR03C, and owning the PostgreSQL schema named auth.
  </description>

  <dependencies>

    <!--
      WHAT: the shared kernel this context builds on, and the only sibling
            module this descriptor is permitted to name.
      WHY : (1) Assumptions: the sibling is built inside this same reactor, so
            its version is by definition the reactor's own, and the property
            below is what keeps the two in lockstep. A literal pin would go on
            resolving to a previously installed artifact after the next version
            bump, which fails quietly at run time instead of loudly at build
            time. (2) Alternatives Considered: depending on any other service
            module was rejected outright, because no service may reach into a
            peer's domain package. That boundary is asserted by the ArchUnit
            layering test that lives in common-lib, so a cross-service edge
            added here would surface as an architecture failure rather than as
            an honest build error.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      WHAT: the framework surface this context compiles against, declared in
            full rather than leaned on transitively.
      WHY : Assumptions: common-lib marks its own starters optional, so nothing
            reaches this module through it. That single fact is why the list
            below is long, and it is deliberate: a library that exported its
            starters would put servlet and persistence types on the classpath of
            every consumer, including consumers that must not have them. The
            web starter in particular is absent from the aggregator's shared
            dependencies for exactly that reason, since placing it there would
            put servlet types on common-lib's own classpath and blunt the
            layering rule that keeps web types out of domain packages.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      WHAT: token validation for the identity provider that replaces the
            baseline's own sign-on check.
      WHY : Refactoring Rationale: the COBOL sign-on read a user record and
            compared an eight character password held in clear text, so the
            credential itself travelled and was stored in the open. This context
            does not carry that field forward at all; it validates a signed token
            and reads the group claim instead, which removes the credential from
            the datastore rather than reproducing it in a new one.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      WHAT: the health surface this module publishes.
      WHY : Assumptions: two independent consumers poll it, the load balancer
            target group and the container image's own health check, so it is a
            deployment contract rather than a convenience and cannot be dropped
            as unused simply because no application code imports it.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
      <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      WHAT: schema migration for the auth schema, as a matched pair.
      WHY : Assumptions: Flyway 10 moved PostgreSQL support out of the core
            artifact into a separate one, so core on its own does not fail the
            build, it fails when the service first opens a connection to a real
            database. That is precisely what makes the second coordinate easy to
            drop and expensive to omit: the mistake is invisible until
            deployment, where it presents as a database the service cannot
            migrate rather than as anything a build would have caught.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      WHAT: the two artifacts this module needs present when it runs but must
            never reference when it compiles.
      WHY : Assumptions: no source file names the driver or the metrics registry.
            The driver is reached through the connection URL and the registry is
            picked up by autoconfiguration, with the common tags contributed by
            common-lib. Keeping both off the compile classpath means an
            accidental direct import of either one fails the build immediately,
            which is the cheapest available guard against a leak of vendor
            specific types into this context.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      WHAT: the container harness behind this module's repository integration
            test.
      WHY : (1) Alternatives Considered: an embedded or in-memory database was
            rejected because V1__auth.sql declares a UUID column and a check
            constraint that restricts the user type to the two values the
            baseline security record allowed. A substitute engine can accept
            that DDL and still enforce it differently, which would leave the
            constraint asserted against something other than the engine that
            actually runs. (2) Trade-offs: the Boot container integration is one
            extra test dependency, accepted because its service connection
            support derives the datasource settings from the container that was
            just started, so the test carries no hand written connection
            properties that could fall out of step with it. (3) Assumptions:
            Testcontainers 2.x renamed every module artifact to carry a
            testcontainers prefix, so the two coordinates below read
            testcontainers-postgresql and testcontainers-junit-jupiter. Their
            1.x names are absent from the Testcontainers BOM the aggregator
            imports, so spelling them the old way would leave both dependencies
            with no managed version and Maven would refuse to read this file.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-testcontainers</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      WHAT: the dependencies this module is required NOT to have. Each absence
            below is a decision with a reasonable alternative, so each is
            recorded here rather than left for a reader to infer from silence.

      Assumptions: the Boot test starter is not named above. The aggregator
        declares it once, at test scope, in its own dependencies element, so it
        already applies to all nine modules. Repeating it would add a second
        declaration whose scope could later be edited independently.

      Alternatives Considered: the ArchUnit test support is not named above
        either. A module local architecture test was considered and rejected
        because layering has exactly one owner, the single layering test in
        common-lib, and every dependency added here brings a further obligation
        to document it under this project's explainability rule.

      Alternatives Considered: no annotation processor for boilerplate
        generation is used. The obvious candidate generates accessors, and
        generated members cannot carry the documentation this project requires
        on every public member; Java records with hand written members give the
        same brevity while remaining documentable.

      Alternatives Considered: no mapping generator is used either. Its current
        published release is a beta, and more decisively the copybook to DTO
        mapping in this migration is not mechanical: it drops filler fields,
        masks an account number to its last four digits, encrypts national and
        government identifiers, and corrects three misspelled baseline field
        names. Every one of those needs a justification recorded at the mapping
        site, which a generated mapper has nowhere to put.

      Alternatives Considered: no cloud SDK and no external configuration
        property source are declared. Configuration arrives as environment
        variables that the task definition injects from Parameter Store and
        Secrets Manager, SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL among them, so the framework's
        own relaxed environment binding already reads it. Adding a property
        source library would pull in an SDK and open a second privileged read
        path for configuration the platform has already resolved before the
        process starts.

      Assumptions: nothing for messaging is declared, because this context
        neither publishes nor consumes a message; queue configuration belongs to
        the authorization and batch contexts alone. The batch starter is absent
        for the same reason, being scoped to the batch context.

      Trade-offs: no retry library and no circuit breaker are declared. Spring
        Framework 7 relocated retry into the framework core, which supplies the
        Retryable and ConcurrencyLimit annotations and a programmatic retry
        policy, switched on by EnableResilientMethods rather than by the older
        enabling annotation, and configured through maxRetries rather than the
        older attribute name, with total attempts being one more than that
        number. The established third party library targets the previous Boot
        major version, and the superseded framework module is redundant. A
        circuit breaker is omitted as a considered trade: the only synchronous
        calls here stay inside the private network behind an internal load
        balancer with bounded timeouts, so a breaker would introduce a new
        failure mode of its own without removing one that exists.

      Assumptions: no cache tier, no streaming platform and no read replica
        routing appear. The baseline has none of them, none is needed to match
        its behaviour, and each would widen this module beyond the migration's
        agreed scope.
    -->

  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <!--
        WHAT: the single plugin this module adds; every other plugin it runs,
              Checkstyle and the two test runners included, is inherited.
        WHY : Alternatives Considered: this plugin is present here and is
              deliberately absent from common-lib. The asymmetry is the point.
              This module is one of the eight deployables, and its Dockerfile
              copies one self contained jar, so it needs the repackage step that
              builds one. Running that same step on a library would move the
              library's classes under BOOT-INF/classes, which is not a location
              a dependent module can put on its classpath, so the library would
              build successfully and then fail to be usable. The finished
              artifact's name is left at the inherited default, because the
              repackage step writes the bootable jar under that name and leaves
              the original beside it under a longer one; the image build selects
              the first by a plain jar glob, and renaming would break that
              selection.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

</project>
